\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{A two-band symbol and repeated blocks}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{Let #{\mathcal B(r)=\sum_{j=1}^3f_j(r)\sigma_j} with the Pauli matrices #{\sigma_j} and a map
#{f:R\to\mathbb R^3} that is nonzero at the vertices of a triangle, on a grid of any dimension
#{n\ge 2}. The negative spectral subspace at a vertex #{v} is spanned by the unit vector
#{\psi_v} with #{(f(v)\cdot\sigma)\psi_v=-|f(v)|\psi_v}, so #{q=1}. The number #{z} of the
[chirality of a closed surface](fdip-015O) is
#{\langle\psi_{v_0},\psi_{v_1}\rangle\langle\psi_{v_1},\psi_{v_2}\rangle\langle\psi_{v_2},\psi_{v_0}\rangle},
and the triangle is regular when this product is not a real number at most #{0}. For an integer #{s\ge 1}, on the
complex space of the operator #{\mathcal B\otimes\bbI_s} the negative subspaces have dimension
#{q=s}. A basis matrix is #{\psi_v\otimes\bbI_s}. Each overlap matrix is the
single scalar overlap times #{\bbI_s}, so its determinant is that scalar's #{s}-th power.
Consequently #{z} is the #{s}-th power of the product above. When the single triangle is regular, write #{\varphi} for its flux. Then the repeated triangle has
undefined flux when #{s\varphi} is an odd multiple of #{\pi}. If it is regular, its flux
is #{s\varphi} exactly when #{|\varphi|<\tfrac{\pi}{s}}. When it is regular and #{|s\varphi|>\pi},
its principal argument differs from #{s\varphi} by a nonzero multiple of #{2\pi}. For example, a phase of
#{\tfrac{3\pi}{4}} doubles to #{-\tfrac{\pi}{2}} on the principal branch.}
